{% import '_common/networkpolicy-macros.j2' as np with context %}
{% import '_common/app-netpol-gate.j2' as gate with context %}
{% if gate.active('keycloakx') %}
{# Keycloak (keycloakx chart). Ingress-only: the namespace egress cannot be listed (identity providers and
   federation endpoints chosen per realm, provider / theme downloads of the init container, HTTP proxy, SMTP,
   Barman backups of pg-cluster-keycloak to S3), so no DNS policy and no Egress side.
   The chart-native NetworkPolicy of the keycloakx chart stays disabled (values/00-main.j2, keycloakx.networkPolicy):
   both sets would add up on the same pods.
   Ingress sources (pods labeled app.kubernetes.io/name=keycloakx by the chart): ingress controller to the http
   port 8080 (Ingress targets the <subDomain>-http Service 80 -> 8080), console to 8080 (in-cluster Service), CNPG
   operator to the pg-cluster-keycloak status port 8000, metrics scraper to the management port 9000, the http
   port 8080 and the pg-cluster-keycloak metrics port 9187. JGroups discovery 7800, PostgreSQL 5432 and the
   post-install job are inside the namespace (intra). Every other client (applications, GitLab, Harbor, SonarQube,
   Argo CD, Vault OIDC, other clusters) uses the public URL through the ingress controller.
   The CNPG operator peer is np.cnpg_operator_peer (local namespace, else global.networkpolicies.cnpgOperatorNamespace,
   else any namespace with the operator pod labels); the scrape rule follows np.scrape_on; ACME HTTP-01 solver pods
   are opened by np.acme_solver. #}
{% set app = 'keycloakx' %}
{% set kc = {'app.kubernetes.io/name': 'keycloakx'} %}
{{ np.intra(app, egress=false) }}
{{ np.acme_solver(app) }}
{{ np.ingress_controller(app, kc, [8080]) }}
{% if gate.installed('console') %}
{{ np.from_namespaces(app, 'console', kc, [{'namespace': np.ns('console'), 'selector': {}}], [8080]) }}
{% endif %}
{{ np.from_namespaces(app, 'cnpg-operator', {'cnpg.io/cluster': 'pg-cluster-keycloak'}, [np.cnpg_operator_peer() | from_json], [8000]) }}
{% if np.scrape_on() %}
{{ np.scrape(app, {}, [8080, 9000, 9187]) }}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
